Word
====

A word processor for Microsoft Word documents (.docx), written in
JavaScript on the disc. It is being built: this version shows Word
documents, lets you type and delete text (with undo), and saves
copies of them. It cannot format text or save over the file yet.

Opening a document
------------------
Double-click a .docx file in a directory display, or drop one on
Word's icon on the icon bar (or on a document's window). Only one
Word runs: more documents open in the same one, each in a window of
its own. Opening a document that is already open brings its window
to the front.

The window shows the text of the document in the fonts
it asks for (or ones with the same widths: Carlito for Calibri,
Caladea for Cambria, Liberation for Arial, Times New Roman and
Courier New). Bold, italic, underlining, colours, sizes, headings,
indents, spacing and centred or right-aligned paragraphs are shown.
The text is as wide as on the document's page (A4 if it does not
say), centred in the window; scroll sideways if the window is
narrower. Info on the window's menu says what is kept but not shown
(tables, pictures, fields, links...) and the fonts used.
Tables are shown as a grey box; pictures and
other things in a line as a small [...] box, links as blue text.
Spelling marks, bookmarks and field codes are not shown at all (a
field shows its result). Other fonts are shown in that font if the
computer has it, else in Liberation Sans.

Click in the text to put the caret there; drag to select (drag past
the window's edge and it scrolls); double-click selects a word and
a third click the paragraph; SHIFT-click or ADJUST-click extends the
selection. The arrow keys move the caret (with CTRL by words and
paragraphs), Home and End (Copy) to the start and end of the line,
CTRL-Home and CTRL-End to the start and end of the document, Page
Up and Page Down by a window; hold SHIFT to select as it moves.
CTRL-A selects everything and Escape ends a selection. MENU never
moves the selection. A table is one box: the caret goes before or
after it, and a double-click selects it. Right-to-left text (Arabic,
Hebrew) is not handled yet: the caret goes through it in stored
order. Nothing can be copied or pasted yet.

Typing
------
Type at the caret: any language, accents, emoji; typing over a
selection replaces it. RETURN starts a new paragraph, SHIFT-RETURN a
new line in it, TAB a tab. BACKSPACE and DELETE delete a character
(CTRL: a word); at the start or end of a paragraph they join it to
its neighbour. Next to a table the first press selects it and the
second deletes it. INSERT switches to overwriting (the character
after the caret is shaded) and back.

CTRL-Z undoes, CTRL-Y or CTRL-SHIFT-Z redoes (1000 steps); Undo and
Redo are also on the Edit submenu of MENU. A word typed with the
spaces after it is one step; RETURN, deleting, a pause, moving the
caret and typing over a selection each start a new one. Input
methods (for Japanese, Chinese...) show what they compose underlined
at the caret. The title shows a * while the document has changes: it
is not saved itself yet (only a copy can be saved), so the * stays.

Not yet: formatting, copy and paste, find, a real Save, typing into
a document with no paragraph, and deleting a section break (deleting
across one leaves the sections as they are). On a Mac use CTRL, not
Cmd, for Z, Y and A. Click in the window first if characters typed
with AltGr or Option are lost.

Saving a copy
-------------
Click MENU over the window and choose "Save copy as .docx": drag the
icon to a directory display, or type a full pathname and click OK.
The copy holds everything the original did, including the parts
that are not shown yet (pictures, tables, footnotes, headers...).

The copy is the document as it is now, changes included. Info
shows what the document holds; Close closes its window. Quit, in
the icon bar menu, stops Word. Neither asks first: changes not
saved in a copy are lost.

If a file cannot be read (it is not a .docx, or it is damaged) Word
says so, naming the file, and carries on.

The program is in this application directory: its files are
JavaScript (file type JSScript) and can be read in !Edit. The fonts
it uses to lay out Word documents are in Fonts, with their licences.
Parts it shares with other applications are in WimpLib, the
library in $.!Boot.Resources.!WimpLib.

The guide to Word is $.Docs.Word.
